# 17. Google Sheets Data Structure

Google Sheets will be the initial data store.

The backend must access the spreadsheet through the Google Sheets API using a dedicated service account.

The frontend must never access the service account credentials directly.

Recommended worksheets:

| Worksheet | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Team | Member profiles, roles and schedules |
| Sprints | Sprint details |
| Tickets | Ticket assignments and progress |
| WorkEntries | Daily work and time tracking |
| DailySubmissions | Daily submission records |
| Holidays | Holiday calendar |
| Leave | Approved leave records |
| ReminderLog | Email delivery history |
| AuditLog | System audit events |
| Settings | Global application configuration |

## 17.1 Data Integrity

- Every record must have a unique identifier.
- Relationships must use stable IDs.
- Dates must use a consistent format.
- Timestamps must be stored in a consistent timezone format.
- API validation must occur before spreadsheet writes.
- Duplicate daily submissions must be prevented.
- Carry-forward operations must be idempotent.
- Audit records must be append-only.
- Spreadsheet edits must not bypass application permissions.

## 17.2 Future Database Migration

The application should use a repository/data-access abstraction.

This allows Google Sheets to be replaced later with PostgreSQL without rewriting the React frontend or the entire business logic.

Google Sheets is suitable for an initial small-team deployment, but concurrency, write limits, audit integrity and reporting complexity should be reviewed as usage increases.

